## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of conditional Terraform blocks, ensuring safe incremental changes and plan stability when toggling a feature flag.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic blocks in Terraform
- Conditional resource configuration
- AWS S3 server-side encryption basics
- Plan drift and backward compatibility

## Code Example
```javascript
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "b" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  dynamic "server_side_encryption_configuration" {
    for_each = var.enable_sse ? [1] : []
    content {
      rule {
        apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
          sse_algorithm = "AES256"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to support SSE with a customer-managed key (KMS) via kms_master_key_id?
- What tests would you add to validate idempotency when toggling the flag across environments?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design multi-cloud infrastructure with consistent module boundaries, robust state management, and policy-driven gates, reflecting real-world enterprise needs.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliases and multiple backends
- Shared module design for multi-cloud resources
- Per-environment state isolation and plan gating
- Drift Detection and policy enforcement (OPA/Sentinel)
- CI/CD integration for plan-only gates

## Code Example

```hcl
# Minimal sketch illustrating two providers with aliases and a multi-env config
provider "aws" {
  region = var.aws_region
  alias  = "aws_prod"
}
provider "google" {
  project = var.gcp_project
  region  = var.gcp_region
  alias   = "gcp_prod"
}

module "messaging" {
  source = "./modules/messaging"
  config = {
    prod = {
      aws  = { region = "us-east-1" }
      gcp  = { project = "prod-project" }
    }
    staging = {
      aws  = { region = "us-west-2" }
      gcp  = { project = "staging-project" }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle secrets rotation across clouds in Terraform?
- What are the trade-offs of using Sentinel vs OPA for this gating scenario?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to implement basic policy enforcement in Terraform using native provider features and CI/CD gates.

## Key Concepts
- AWS provider default_tags for universal tag propagation across all resources
- Wrapper module to enforce consistent environment context
- Per-environment backend configuration in S3
- GitHub Actions gate using terraform plan output to detect policy violations

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = var.region
  default_tags {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      CostCenter  = var.cost_center
      Owner       = var.owner
    }
  }
}
```
